title: Referential integrity of a forest
tags: [[TreeRef forest]]
kind: reference
summary: A position's parent must exist in the same forest, a position with children cannot be taken away, and the repository root cannot be deleted.

A `tree_ref` names its parent by uuid, and the daemon holds both ends of that
reference on every manual write:

* ''Creating'' a position: the parent must be a metarecord that itself has a position ''in the same forest''. One that does not exist, or exists in another forest only, is refused with `400` (`invalid TreeRef parent …: no such metarecord carrying a 'path' TreeRef field`).
* ''Removing'' one: a position may not be taken away while metarecords are placed under it — refused with `400` naming the first children (`cannot remove the last 'path' position of …: 2 metarecord(s) are placed under it ("jazz", "rock"); move or delete them first`). This covers every shape that takes a position away: `unset`, deleting the row, setting the field to `nothing` or to another value, a whole-record overwrite, deleting the metarecord.

That second check runs ''once, against the state the revision is about to commit'',
not per operation: deleting a whole subtree in one revision is fine even though the
parent goes first; only the committed state must be a forest.

One position can never be removed: the ''filesystem root'', the `mfr_path` node with
no parent and an empty name. It anchors every path; a repository without it resolves
nothing, and recreating it is no repair — a new metarecord has a new uuid, while the
whole forest names the old one. Deleting it — `DELETE …/metarecords/:uuid`,
`POST …/query/delete`, or trashing it ([[POST /repos/:repo/metarecords/trash]]) — is
refused, children or not. A root deleted before the check existed comes back by a
rollback or a revert of its deletion, which restores it at its own uuid.

''Neither cascade nor silence.'' Removing everything under a vanished directory is
what the ''watcher'' does for `mfr_path`, where the filesystem is authoritative and
the directory really is gone (guarded by the `orphan-cascade-limit` circuit breaker,
[[Daemon configuration]]). A manual write has no such authority: it is refused rather
than allowed to erase a subtree the caller did not name. So the watcher's operations
(`file_deleted`, `file_moved`, `file_modified`) are exempt, and so is log navigation,
which restores rows directly and passes through intermediate states on its way to one
that was valid when written.

Before the check existed (September 2026), taking a parent's position away left its
children ''detached'': reachable by uuid, but under no parent and in no list of roots.
A repository written before then may still hold some; nothing lists them today.
